[38;5;252m[1m## The library[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mc_src[0m is a single 36-line file exporting two functions, [38;5;10mdriver(char)[0m and [38;5;10mprintHexCharLine(char)[0m. Both return [38;5;10mvoid[0m and communicate only by [38;5;10mprintf[0m-ing to stdout. The C has zero branches, zero [38;5;10mreturn[0ms, zero asserts, and no pointer/length/enum parameters — so it has no error surface at all.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## Bug found and fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mprintHexCharLine[0m was not ABI-behaviour-identical, even though the symbol names matched from the start. GCC compiles the C callee to re-narrow its argument register ([38;5;10mmov %al,-0x4(%rbp)[0m then [38;5;10mmovsbl[0m), while Rust's [38;5;10mextern "C" fn(c_char)[0m compiled to a bare [38;5;10mmov %edi,%esi[0m — the ABI lets the callee assume the caller already narrowed. So [38;5;10mprintHexCharLine(0x100)[0m printed [38;5;10m00[0m from C and [38;5;10m100[0m from Rust.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Fix: declare both exports as taking [38;5;10mc_int[0m (ABI-identical, same register) and truncate explicitly, reproducing GCC's [38;5;10mmov %al[0m + [38;5;10mmovsbl[0m. [38;5;10mprintHexCharLine[0m now compiles to [38;5;10mmovsbl %dil,%esi[0m. [38;5;10mdriver[0m was already correct — [38;5;10minc %dil[0m only touches the low byte.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This was caught by exactly one of the 20 test rows, the one that passes full-width [38;5;10mint[0m values through the widened [38;5;10mvoid f(int)[0m signature — the FFI analogue of an out-of-range enum value.[0m[0m
